@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Controller Rules";
}

@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}

<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW14000 </span> USE Post/Redirect/Get (PRG) when submitting forms (exclude case using ajax) <span class="lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>Here are the examples:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>HTTP GET of “/products/create”. “Create” view is rendered.</li>
                <li>HTTP POST to “/products/submit”.</li>
                <li>Validation Fails, redirect to “products/create”. “Create” view is rendered.</li>
                <li>HTTP POST to “/products/submit”.</li>
                <li>Item is create, redirect to “/products/confirm”.”Confirm” view is rendered.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW14001 </span> KEEP controllers free from HttpContext and its tail <span class="lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                If you need to access any information from HttpContext, you can use custom filter or create some
                interfaces and wrappers to pass in your controller. You can see it in the following example:
            </p>
            <p>We have a controller method is declared as:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    [AcceptVerbs(HttpVerbs.Get), OutputCache(CacheProfile = "Dashboard"), UserNameFilter]
                    public ActionResult Dashboard(string userName, StoryListTab tab, OrderBy orderBy, int? page)
                    {
                        return View();
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>The UsernameFilter is responsible for passing the Username:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public class UsernameFilter : ActionFilterAttribute
                    {
                        public override void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext filterContext)
                        {
                            const string Key = "userName";
                            if (filterContext.ActionParameters.ContainsKey(Key))
                            {
                                if (filterContext.HttpContext.User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
                                {
                                    filterContext.ActionParameters[Key] = filterContext.HttpContext.User.Identity.Name;
                                }
                            }
                            base.OnActionExecuting(filterContext);
                        }
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW14002 </span> USE model binding instead of manually parsing the request <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                ASP.NET MVC abstracts much of the rote code of object deserialization by using model binding.
                Model binding is a mechanism by which request context data is marshaled through reflection into the
                object type defined on the action method.
            </p>
            <p>Here is the following example: We have User class.</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public class User
                    {
                        private string _username;
                        public string Username
                        {
                            get
                            {
                                return string.IsNullOrEmpty(_username) ? string.Empty : _username;
                            }
                            set
                            {
                                _username = value;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>And a view model class:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public class ManageUserViewModel
                    {
                        private List&lt;user&gt; _userList;
                        public ManageUserViewModel()
                        {
                            _userList = new List&lt;user&gt;();
                        }
                        public List&lt;user&gt; UserList
                        {
                            get
                            {
                                return _userList;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>Then we have an action method which calling above view model:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public ActionResult ManageUser()
                    {
                        var userList = _userService.GetAllUsers();
                        var model = new ManageUserViewModel();
                        model.UserList.AddRange(userList);
                        return View(model);
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>
                The model binding system also runs the validation logic that has been applied to the model object,
                such as the data annotations attributes. It also has a rich extensibility mechanism that allows full
                customization of how object are created, populated and validated.
            </p>
        </li>        
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW14003 </span> IMPLEMENT HandleUnknownAction and HandleError <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                The default response for the unknown action is 404 (Not Found) error. If we override the HandleUnknownAction
                class in a Controller, we can implement a “default” view in this error. Additionally, put the HandleError
                attribute on an action and/ or Controller and we can provide a standard error view for when a uncaught
                exception is thrown. Then, here is the example:
            </p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public class BaseController : Controller
                    {
                        protected override void HandleUnknownAction(string actionName)
                        {
                            var strError = string.Format("Unable to find action {0}", actionName);
                            Logger.Log(strError);
                            throw new ObjectNotFoundException(strError);
                        }
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>        
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW14004 </span> KEEP your controller to be a single responsibility <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                All methods you can put in your controller is for validating input, calling model while preparing view
                and return view or redirect to another action.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
